WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:09.180
In 1928, Alexander Fleming discovered that a fungus contaminating his petri dishes

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:13.800
had released a powerful antibiotic that killed the surrounding bacteria.

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:17.400
He named this antibiotic “penicillin”.

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:24.660
Nearly two decades later, after years of intense cooperation between scientists, manufacturers,

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:28.900
and government agencies, penicillin was made 
broadly available.

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:35.760
In 1945, using X-ray crystallography, Dorothy
Hodgkin determined the chemical structure

00:00:35.770 --> 00:00:40.200
of penicillin, a discovery that fueled the
quest to synthesize the drug.

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:51.100
In 1952, a form of penicillin suitable for
oral use - called Penicillin V - was developed.

00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:58.660
And in 1957, penicillin V was produced synthetically
for the first time, laying the foundation

00:00:58.660 --> 00:01:02.700
for the development and synthesis of new
penicillin antibiotics.

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:07.040
Penicillin is effective for treating bacterial
infections

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:09.300
because it disrupts an essential process in the bacterial life cycle:

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:13.000
the creation of the bacterial cell wall.

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:22.940
The cell wall in most bacteria consists of
a lipid bilayer membrane

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:25.300
and a mesh-like peptidoglycan layer.

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:31.500
Gram-positive bacteria build a thick peptidoglycan sheath around a single membrane,

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:37.400
while Gram-negative bacteria typically build a thin layer of peptidoglycan between two membranes.

00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:43.900
Water constantly enters bacterial cells by
osmosis,

00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:45.950
building up pressure on the cell membrane.

00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:48.800
Peptidoglycan allows the cell to resist this
pressure

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:51.000
by providing structural support forthe membrane.

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:54.800
Penicillin prevents bacteria from building
their peptidoglycan layer,

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:58.300
causing the bacteria to burst under pressure.

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:05.800
Let’s take a closer look at how penicillin
acts at the molecular level.

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:12.200
Peptidoglycan is made up of small building
blocks, each composed of two sugars

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:14.400
connected to a short chain of amino acids

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:17.000
with a peptide bridge 
extending to the side.

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:21.500
These sugars are assembled into chains, which
are then crosslinked via the peptide bridges

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:24.000
to form a tough peptidoglycan matrix.

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:34.870
The enzyme D-alanyl-D-alanine carboxypeptidase/transpeptidase,
also known as Penicillin-Binding Protein,

00:02:34.870 --> 00:02:40.720
assists with peptidoglycan matrix assembly
by creating the crosslinks between the chains.

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:49.370
Penicillin antibiotics block this enzyme by
making a direct bond to a key serine amino

00:02:49.370 --> 00:02:51.000
acid in its active site.

00:02:51.590 --> 00:02:54.300
The active portion of penicillin is a beta-lactam ring.

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:58.000
It is chemically reactive,
and opens up to form a bond

00:02:58.000 --> 00:02:59.330
to the active site serine.

00:02:59.330 --> 00:03:05.000
This inactivates the enzyme and prevents proper
formation of the peptidoglycan matrix.

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:10.940
The beta-lactam ring is found in both natural
and synthetic antibiotics

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:13.640
that are structurally similar to penicillin.

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:18.090
Beta-lactam antibiotics are effective against
many types of bacterial infections, and have

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:20.750
helped to save countless human lives.

00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:25.511
However, bacterial communities are living
things, and like any other living thing,

00:03:25.511 --> 00:03:27.170
they adapt to survive.

00:03:27.170 --> 00:03:32.960
To protect themselves from antibiotics, bacteria
developed resistance mechanisms.

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:38.710
For example, MRSA, a strain of Staphylococcus
aureus, expresses Penicillin Binding Protein 2a,

00:03:38.710 --> 00:03:44.300
which has an altered active site that
doesn’t bind beta-lactam antibiotics.

00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:56.770
Bacteria can also express special Beta-lactamase
enzymes, which bind to beta-lactam antibiotics

00:03:56.770 --> 00:04:02.600
and break the essential beta-lactam ring,
making the antibiotic ineffective.

00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:12.740
Medical scientists worked quickly to develop
new drugs to fight resistance.

00:04:12.740 --> 00:04:17.100
Some of these drugs block beta-lactamase directly,
leaving the antibiotic free

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:19.600
to inhibit Penicillin-Binding proteins.

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:24.280
Other new beta-lactam drugs are not 
recognized or

00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:27.380
not broken down by some beta-lactamases.

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:37.600
Unfortunately, in the face of overuse and
misuse of antibiotics, bacteria continue to

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:40.420
develop ways to resist the action of new drugs.

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:46.260
Many genes that are crucial in antibiotic
resistance are encoded on small circular pieces

00:04:46.270 --> 00:04:48.639
of DNA called plasmids.

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:53.139
These plasmids can be passed from one population
of bacteria to another, and then from generation

00:04:53.140 --> 00:04:55.340
to generation.

00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:02.620
One such particularly dangerous gene is NDM-1.

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:12.060
Bacteria possessing this gene can build the
New Delhi metallo-beta-lactamase enzyme.

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:15.900
These types of enzymes can break down almost
all known beta-lactam drugs,

00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:18.080
posing a major global health threat.

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:25.460
Today, less than 100 years since Fleming’s
discovery, we are in the middle of an

00:05:25.460 --> 00:05:28.219
arms race with pathogenic bacteria.

00:05:28.219 --> 00:05:32.240
Using our growing knowledge of the structural
biology of bacterial enzymes, the medical

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:36.500
community is looking for new ways to target
the weak points of bacteria.

